1. X - Ø = {a, b, c, d}
2. X - {a} = {b, c, d}
3. X - {a, b, c, d} = Ø

The closed sets are {a, b, c, d}, {b, c, d}, and Ø. The smallest closed set containing {b, c} is {b, c, d}.

Therefore, the closure of the set {b, c} in the topological space X is {b, c, d}.

A topology on a set X is a collection of subsets of X, called open sets, that satisfy the following three conditions:

1. The empty set and the set X itself are open sets.
2. The intersection of any finite number of open sets is an open set.
3. The union of any collection of open sets is an open set.

